packages/devbox/tests/strategy-conformance.test.ts:

smart-container bar — per-arm matrix (design § 6)

cell    snapshot-chain  
6.1     existing          attach empty, write, commit, replace, attach
6.2     existing          quiesce publishes exactly once
6.3     existing          three generations, each wake carries every commit
6.4     existing          death at every commit seam serves old or new
6.6     existing          control metadata outside every payload prefix
6.7     existing          corrupted payload refused by name
6.8     existing          commit interrupted by replacement converges
6.16    existing          teardown after stop is classified
6.9     pass              DO reset mid-restore: one daemon, one restore, tree exact
6.10    pass              container replaced mid-commit, old boot finishes late
6.11    pass              byte-for-byte: mode, owner, times, xattrs, symlink, hardlink, sparse
6.12    pass              counted bounds, and the same k against n and 10n
6.13    pass              1e5 files: exact tree, same RestoreWork as 1e3 files
6.14    refused           1 GiB sparse plus 64 MiB dense: commit O(data), wake O(1), in-place seal O(k)
6.15    pass              sqlite rewrite: random 4 KiB pwrites, bytesPut bounded by dirty pages
6.17    pass              two containers racing: one head, loser refused and reported, never merged
6.18    pass              disk full mid-journal: ENOSPC, no effect without a record, eviction, tree exact
6.20    pass              GC never deletes a reachable object
6.21    pass              restore and backup time versus tree size at three sizes
6.22    pass              C3 overwrite: a 64 KiB edit publishes below 196,608 bytes in exactly one object
6.23    pass              many small changed files: one object per checkpoint, bytes near the change
6.24    pass              per-file maps: a one-file change costs the same at 1,000 and 5,000 files
6.19    harness           stop then wake on the same instance: the devbox-harness suites

6.14 snapshot-chain: refused — a snapshot-chain wake makes 4 remote ops against the O(1) bound of 3: the base integrity head, the head that adopts an unreferenced delta, the store mount's list and the base layer's get (a base+delta wake makes 5; measured 2026-09-10 in the conformance harness)

snapshot-chain 6.22: bytesPut=90298 objectsPut=1
snapshot-chain 6.23: bytesPut=184091 objectsPut=1
snapshot-chain 6.24: files=1000 bytesPut=877 objectsPut=1 attachBytes=877 attachObjects=1
snapshot-chain 6.24: files=5000 bytesPut=877 objectsPut=1 attachBytes=877 attachObjects=1
6.21 restore and backup time versus tree size — 100, 1,000 and 10,000 files of 4 KiB

arm snapshot-chain: files | tree bytes | full backup ms | 64 KiB backup ms | 64 KiB backup bytesPut | restore ms | restore ops | restore payload bytes
